15 0 obj
<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Person wearing a fNIRS sensor/headband and attached physiological sensors sits in a flight simulator cockpit, operating controls and viewing a monitor with aircraft instrument data and a large wraparound screen displaying an aerial landscape.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 28 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Flowchart diagram illustrating a multi-step process for feature preselection, model evaluation, and result selection using cognitive task scores and stress resilience features as inputs, with outputs of mean absolute error and Pearson correlation. The chart uses color-coded boxes and arrows, divides procedures into two main tracks with feature grouping, subset selection, evaluation using RMSE, and highlights three cases for result selection leading to different trained model applications. Mathematical notation and dashed lines clarify grouping, decision points, and workflow sequence.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 31 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Six-panel scatter plot graphic comparing predicted versus actual simulator performance using different regression models under a fixed domain-balanced feature strategy. Each panel shows blue dots, a diagonal dashed line, and metrics: mean absolute error \(MAE\), normalized MAE, and correlation coefficient \(r\). Models displayed include MRA, Ridge, Bayesian Ridge, SVR linear, RegNN, and Bagging NN, with RegNN achieving the lowest MAE and highest correlation.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Bar chart titled \215Domain-level ablation: change in normalized MAE\216 comparing changes in normalized mean absolute error \(MAE\) across domains Resting, Startle, Allostatic, and Cognitive for six methods: MRA, Ridge, SVR-linear, GBM, RegNN, and Bagging NN. Startle and Allostatic domains show highest changes, especially for Bagging NN and GBM, while Resting and Cognitive domains show lower changes for all methods.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
